SURVEY REPORT

Title: Survey Report on Types of Blockchain and Their Real-Time Use Cases

1. Aim

To study different types of blockchain technology and understand how blockchain is used in real life in different fields such as banking, healthcare, education, business, and government services.

2. Introduction

Blockchain is a modern technology used to store data and transaction records securely. It is a type of digital record book that is shared among many computers.

In blockchain, information is stored in blocks. These blocks are connected to each other using cryptographic hashes. Every block contains transaction details, a timestamp, and the hash of the previous block.

Once information is added to the blockchain, it becomes very difficult to change or delete it. This makes blockchain a secure and trusted technology.

Blockchain provides many important features:

- Security: It protects data from unauthorized changes.
- Transparency: Users can verify transactions according to network permissions.
- Decentralization: Data is not controlled by only one person or organization in a public blockchain.
- Immutability: Stored records are difficult to modify.
- Trust: Users can verify information without always depending on a middle person.

Blockchain uses methods called consensus mechanisms to verify transactions. Two common methods are Proof of Work (PoW) and Proof of Stake (PoS).

Today, blockchain is used in banking, healthcare, education, supply chain management, digital identity, insurance, and many other fields.

3. Types of Blockchain

There are four main types of blockchain:

3.1 Public Blockchain

A public blockchain is a network that is open to everyone. Any person can join the network, view transactions, and participate in the validation process according to the network rules.

It does not depend on one central organization. All participating users can verify the transactions.

Features:

1. Anyone can join the network.
2. Transactions are transparent.
3. No single organization controls the entire network.
4. It provides good security through cryptography and consensus.
5. It may be slower when many transactions are processed.

Examples:

- Bitcoin
- Ethereum

Real-life use: Bitcoin is used for digital currency transactions, while Ethereum supports cryptocurrency and smart contracts.

3.2 Private Blockchain

A private blockchain is a blockchain network controlled by one organization. Only selected and authorized users can join and access the network.

It is mainly used by companies and business organizations to store and share important information securely.

Features:

1. Only authorized users can access the network.
2. A single organization manages the network.
3. Transactions can be processed quickly.
4. It provides better control over data access.
5. It is useful for private business information.

Examples:

- Hyperledger Fabric private deployments
- Private Corda networks

Real-life use: A company can use a private blockchain to maintain employee records, business transactions, and internal data.

3.3 Consortium Blockchain

A consortium blockchain is managed by a group of organizations instead of a single organization.

In this type, only selected organizations can participate in the network and validate transactions. It is useful when multiple organizations need to share information securely.

Features:

1. It is controlled by multiple organizations.
2. Only selected members can join.
3. Organizations share responsibility.
4. It provides better speed and efficiency in many permissioned setups.
5. It is useful for banking, supply chains, and business networks.

Examples:

- IBM Food Trust
- Consortium-based Hyperledger Fabric networks

Real-life use: Different banks can use a consortium blockchain to share and verify financial transaction records.

3.4 Hybrid Blockchain

A hybrid blockchain combines the features of public and private blockchains.

It allows organizations to keep important information private while sharing selected information publicly. This provides both privacy and transparency.

Features:

1. It combines public and private blockchain features.
2. Sensitive data can be kept private.
3. Selected records can be made public.
4. It provides flexible access control.
5. It is useful for organizations that need both privacy and transparency.

Examples:

- Dragonchain
- XinFin (XDC Network)

Real-life use: A company can keep its private business details secure and allow customers to verify selected transaction records.

4. Comparison of Different Types of Blockchain

Parameter| Public| Private| Consortium| Hybrid
Access| Open to everyone| Restricted| Selected members| Public and private
Control| Decentralized| Single organization| Multiple organizations| Mixed
Transparency| High| Limited| Shared| Flexible
Speed| Generally lower| Generally high| Generally high| Depends on design
Privacy| Less privacy for public records| Better access control| Shared access control| Flexible privacy
